%N A090921 Smallest prime of the form concatenation n, 2n, 3n,...kn and 1.
%C A090921 Next term a(26) has 116 digits; k = 39 and is too large to include. - _David Wasserman_, Feb 15 2006
%C A090921 From _Jianing Song_, Aug 30 2026: (Start)
%C A090921 a(35) is likely to be 3570...267401 (a probable prime with 3506 digits), where 26740 = 35*764.
%C A090921 a(67), if it exists, is larger than 67134...804001, where 80400 = 67*1200. (End)
%H A090921 Jianing Song, <a href="/A090921/b090921.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..34</a>
%o A090921 (PARI) a(n) = my(v=[], p); for(k=1, oo, v=concat(v, digits(k*n)); p=fromdigits(v)*10+1; if(ispseudoprime(p), return(p))) \\ _Jianing Song_, Aug 30 2026
